Musix uses Google Material Symbols, licensed under Apache-2.0.
The license is provided in licenses/MaterialSymbols-LICENSE.txt.
Icons originate from https://github.com/google/material-design-icons.
Icons use the official Material Symbols Rounded SVG assets at the 20px, 24px
and 40px optical sizes, FILL=1, with the FILL=0 forms for the symbols that
report a state: the navigation destinations, the liked song and the pinned
collection.
Spun application code is not incorporated into Musix.

Visual references: Google Material Design 3 and Spun.
Architecture reference: https://github.com/amiralibg/unstream.
No Unstream source code is incorporated.

Runtime dependencies carry their own licenses. Qt is dynamically linked;
Qt licensing: https://www.qt.io/licensing/open-source-lgpl-obligations
The installed Qt Multimedia and FFmpeg packages determine the media backend.
Catalog requests use ytmusicapi (MIT); audio resolution uses yt-dlp (Unlicense).

The macOS application bundle redistributes further works, and carries the full
text of each of their licenses in Contents/Resources/licenses:

  FFmpeg-LGPL-2.1.txt      FFmpeg
  CPython-PSF.txt          CPython
  yt-dlp-Unlicense.txt     yt-dlp
  ytmusicapi-MIT.txt       ytmusicapi
  MaterialSymbols-LICENSE.txt  Material Symbols

FFmpeg (https://ffmpeg.org), used unmodified as the ffmpeg and ffprobe
executables, under the GNU Lesser General Public License version 2.1 or later.
The bundled build is configured with --disable-gpl --disable-nonfree
--disable-autodetect, so it contains no GPL-licensed or non-free components and
links no external libraries. scripts/build-ffmpeg.sh reproduces it exactly from
the upstream release it pins. As the LGPL requires, the corresponding source is
published alongside each release as
Musix-<version>-ffmpeg-<ffmpeg version>-source.tar.xz, which is the unmodified
tarball the bundled binaries were built from.

CPython (https://python.org), redistributed as a relocatable build from
python-build-standalone (https://github.com/astral-sh/python-build-standalone),
under the Python Software Foundation License.

yt-dlp (Unlicense) and ytmusicapi (MIT) are installed into that runtime, and
the application updates those two packages in place.

No fonts are bundled. Musix uses an installed Google Sans Flex or Noto Sans font.
Musix is independent of Google and YouTube.
